8
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:29.188
How the grocery platform survives disasters, backups, replication, failover, chaos testing and graceful degradation.

9
00:00:29.468 --> 00:00:40.561
Two labs run real PostgreSQL 16 point-in-time recovery and streaming replication with failover; the others inject real faults and run a real restore drill.

10
00:00:40.841 --> 00:00:42.911
L1: point-in-time recovery.

11
00:00:43.191 --> 00:00:58.081
A DELETE with no WHERE destroyed 57,000 orders; PITR replayed the base backup + archived WAL to a named restore point and recovered all 57,000 (RPO 0) in 335 ms.

12
00:00:59.431 --> 00:01:01.607
L2: replication and failover.

13
00:01:01.887 --> 00:01:19.210
Async replication left a ~16 MB WAL lag window (the RPO on a sudden primary loss) but fast commits; synchronous replication gave RPO 0 at the cost of commit latency; promoting the standby took ~115 ms.

14
00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.389
L3: chaos engineering.

15
00:01:21.669 --> 00:01:25.295
A non-critical dependency made slow or erroring or crashed.

16
00:01:25.575 --> 00:01:38.674
Naive breached the SLO on every fault (p99 3,006 ms; 100% errors when crashed); resilient (timeout + breaker + fallback) held the SLO through all of them.

17
00:01:40.024 --> 00:01:42.499
L4: restore drills and degradation.

18
00:01:42.779 --> 00:01:48.475
An automated drill caught a truncated backup and an orphaned backup that both reported success.

19
00:01:48.755 --> 00:01:56.392
A degradation ladder kept checkout working through the loss of all 3 non-critical dependencies; only a core one took it down.

20
00:01:56.672 --> 00:01:57.653
Reading time:

21
00:01:57.933 --> 00:01:59.853
120 min + code.

22
00:02:01.203 --> 00:02:02.057
Part 1.

23
00:02:02.337 --> 00:02:04.257
Introduction and Why It Matters.

24
00:02:05.507 --> 00:02:07.384
A story: the backup that wasn't.

25
00:02:07.664 --> 00:02:11.909
A failed storage upgrade corrupted the orders database at 2 a.m.

26
00:02:12.189 --> 00:02:24.200
The on-call ran the documented restore, and discovered the nightly backup had been silently truncating for three weeks (a full disk on the backup host), and the last good backup was 22 days old.

27
00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:35.104
Worse, nobody had ever restored a backup, so the runbook's steps were wrong and the WAL archive needed for point-in-time recovery had been pruned by a retention job.

28
00:02:35.384 --> 00:02:38.968
The store was down for eleven hours and lost a day of orders.

29
00:02:39.248 --> 00:02:42.149
The review found the reliability program was theatre:

30
00:02:43.499 --> 00:02:45.035
Backups were never tested.

31
00:02:45.315 --> 00:02:48.238
We have backups was true; we can restore was false.

32
00:02:48.518 --> 00:02:52.230
A restore drill would have caught the truncation weeks earlier (L4).

33
00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.259
No point-in-time recovery.

34
00:02:54.539 --> 00:03:03.286
Even a good backup was a full nightly, so a mid-day disaster meant losing up to a day, no way to recover to just before the corruption (L1).

35
00:03:03.566 --> 00:03:06.553
The replica was async and nobody knew the RPO.

36
00:03:06.833 --> 00:03:13.766
The standby lagged the primary; on failover, in-flight commits would be lost, and the team couldn't state how many (L2).

37
00:03:15.116 --> 00:03:17.377
Failover was manual and unpracticed.

38
00:03:17.657 --> 00:03:21.817
Promoting the standby took an hour of googling, because it had never been drilled.

39
00:03:22.097 --> 00:03:24.849
A single dependency could take everything down.

40
00:03:25.129 --> 00:03:34.665
There was no graceful degradation, when recommendations was slow, the whole product page was slow (L3), and there was no plan for what to shed under stress.

41
00:03:34.945 --> 00:03:49.068
The rebuild: tested backups with PITR, replication with a known RPO and practiced failover, chaos experiments that prove resilience before an incident, and a degradation catalogue so the platform bends instead of breaking.

42
00:03:50.418 --> 00:03:51.101
Quote:

43
00:03:51.381 --> 00:03:53.045
The thesis of this chapter.

44
00:03:53.325 --> 00:04:03.906
Reliability is not the absence of failure, failure is constant, it's the ability to survive failure without losing data or availability beyond your stated targets.

45
00:04:04.186 --> 00:04:17.818
Those targets are RPO (how much data you can lose) and RTO (how long you can be down), and everything else follows: backups and replication set the RPO, failover and restore drills set the RTO.

46
00:04:18.098 --> 00:04:24.114
A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a plan; a failover you have never practiced is a gamble.

47
00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:32.245
And you find out whether your resilience actually works by deliberately breaking things (chaos engineering), not by waiting for the outage.

48
