97
00:06:33.501 --> 00:06:34.375
Part 2.

98
00:06:34.655 --> 00:06:36.533
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

99
00:06:37.783 --> 00:06:38.700
Intuition.

100
00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:43.972
RPO is how much work can you afford to lose, RTO is how long can you be shut.

101
00:06:44.252 --> 00:06:49.777
A bank's ledger tolerates near-zero of both; a recommendations cache tolerates a lot.

102
00:06:50.057 --> 00:06:55.924
You buy down each with money and complexity, so you set them per data tier, not one-size-fits-all.

103
00:06:56.204 --> 00:07:00.492
A backup is a parachute you pack but never jump with, until the day you must.

104
00:07:00.772 --> 00:07:03.161
If you've never opened it, you don't know it works.

105
00:07:03.441 --> 00:07:06.129
The restore drill is the practice jump (L4).

106
00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:11.639
Sync versus async replication is express versus regular post.

107
00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:18.191
Synchronous: the sender waits for the recipient to confirm before considering it done (nothing lost, but slower).

108
00:07:18.471 --> 00:07:25.383
Asynchronous: send and move on (fast, but the last few letters may be in transit when disaster strikes) (L2).

109
00:07:25.663 --> 00:07:28.586
Chaos engineering is a fire drill for software.

110
00:07:28.866 --> 00:07:35.565
You don't wait for a real fire to learn the exits are locked; you set a controlled one and watch what happens (L3).

111
00:07:36.915 --> 00:07:39.027
RPO, RTO, SLA.

112
00:07:39.307 --> 00:07:47.115
RPO (Recovery Point Objective): the maximum data loss you'll accept, measured in time, at most 5 minutes of orders.

113
00:07:47.395 --> 00:07:50.979
It's set by how often you back up and how your replication works.

114
00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:57.360
PITR + sync replication, then near-zero; nightly backups only, then up to 24 h.

115
00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:04.083
RTO (Recovery Time Objective): the maximum time to restore service, back up within 30 minutes.

116
00:08:04.363 --> 00:08:08.032
Set by restore speed, failover automation, and practice.

117
00:08:09.382 --> 00:08:18.299
SLA versus SLO (Ch 24): the SLA is the external promise (with penalties); the SLO is the internal target.

118
00:08:18.579 --> 00:08:20.969
Availability is often quoted in nines:

119
00:08:21.249 --> 00:08:29.206
99.9% about 8.8 h per year down, 99.99% about 52 min per year.

120
00:08:29.486 --> 00:08:42.073
They cost money and complexity, so tier them: payments or orders get strict RPO or RTO (sync replication, fast automated failover); analytics gets loose (nightly backups are fine).

121
00:08:42.353 --> 00:08:44.849
L1 and L2 measure both directly.

122
00:08:46.199 --> 00:08:47.479
Backup strategies.

123
00:08:47.759 --> 00:08:49.103
Here is a quick table:

124
00:08:49.383 --> 00:08:51.409
Type, and What, and Restore, and Cost.

125
00:08:51.689 --> 00:08:52.372
Full.

126
00:08:52.652 --> 00:08:53.207
What:

127
00:08:53.487 --> 00:08:55.065
Everything, periodically.

128
00:08:55.345 --> 00:08:56.220
Restore:

129
00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:58.313
Simplest, slowest to take.

130
00:08:58.593 --> 00:08:59.319
Cost:

131
00:08:59.599 --> 00:09:00.665
Storage-heavy.

132
00:09:00.945 --> 00:09:01.948
Incremental.

133
00:09:02.228 --> 00:09:02.783
What:

134
00:09:03.063 --> 00:09:05.196
Only changes since the last backup.

135
00:09:05.476 --> 00:09:06.351
Restore:

136
00:09:06.631 --> 00:09:09.084
Restore full + each increment in order.

137
00:09:09.364 --> 00:09:10.089
Cost:

138
00:09:10.369 --> 00:09:12.844
Cheap to take, slower or riskier restore.

139
00:09:13.124 --> 00:09:14.127
Differential.

140
00:09:14.407 --> 00:09:14.961
What:

141
00:09:15.241 --> 00:09:16.841
Changes since the last full.

142
00:09:17.121 --> 00:09:17.996
Restore:

143
00:09:18.276 --> 00:09:20.303
Restore full + one differential.

144
00:09:20.583 --> 00:09:21.308
Cost:

145
00:09:21.588 --> 00:09:22.569
Middle ground.

146
00:09:22.849 --> 00:09:25.580
Continuous (WAL or log archiving).

147
00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:26.415
What:

148
00:09:26.695 --> 00:09:28.871
Every change, streamed to archive.

149
00:09:29.151 --> 00:09:30.025
Restore:

150
00:09:30.305 --> 00:09:32.268
Enables point-in-time recovery.

151
00:09:32.548 --> 00:09:33.273
Cost:

152
00:09:33.553 --> 00:09:35.324
The basis of low RPO.

153
00:09:36.674 --> 00:09:43.607
Point-in-time recovery (PITR) = a base (full) backup + a continuous archive of the write-ahead log.

154
00:09:43.887 --> 00:09:48.965
To recover, restore the base backup and replay the WAL up to a chosen instant.

155
00:09:49.245 --> 00:10:10.237
L1 (real PostgreSQL): a DELETE destroyed 57,000 orders; PITR restored the 39 MB base backup and replayed the archived WAL to a named restore point created just before the mistake, recovering all 57,000 rows (RPO 0) in 335 ms.

156
00:10:10.517 --> 00:10:16.127
Without WAL archiving you could only restore the base backup (losing everything since it).

157
00:10:17.477 --> 00:10:19.269
The 3 to 2-1 rule:

158
00:10:19.549 --> 00:10:27.101
3 copies, on 2 different media, 1 off-site (and increasingly, 1 immutable or air-gapped copy against ransomware).

159
00:10:27.381 --> 00:10:35.275
Backups must also be encrypted (Ch 23) and have tested retention aligned to compliance (Ch 36).

160
00:10:35.555 --> 00:10:38.755
And critically, tested (§2.6, L4).

161
00:10:40.105 --> 00:10:42.643
Replication for availability and DR.

162
00:10:42.923 --> 00:10:53.505
Replication (Ch 10) keeps a live copy so you can fail over quickly (short RTO) and lose little (short RPO), unlike restoring a backup (long RTO).

163
00:10:53.785 --> 00:10:56.430
The sync or async choice decides the RPO:

164
00:10:57.780 --> 00:10:59.124
Here is a quick table:

165
00:10:59.404 --> 00:11:04.652
Mode, and Commit waits for the replica?, and RPO on sudden primary loss, and Cost.

166
00:11:04.932 --> 00:11:05.956
Asynchronous.

167
00:11:06.236 --> 00:11:07.836
Commit waits for the replica?:

168
00:11:08.116 --> 00:11:08.799
No.

169
00:11:09.079 --> 00:11:11.105
RPO on sudden primary loss:

170
00:11:11.385 --> 00:11:14.415
The replication lag window (in-flight commits lost).

171
00:11:14.695 --> 00:11:15.420
Cost:

172
00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:17.855
Fast commits; replica may lag.

173
00:11:18.135 --> 00:11:19.031
Synchronous.

174
00:11:19.311 --> 00:11:20.911
Commit waits for the replica?:

175
00:11:21.191 --> 00:11:23.751
Yes (replica must persist the WAL).

176
00:11:24.031 --> 00:11:26.057
RPO on sudden primary loss:

177
00:11:26.337 --> 00:11:27.084
Zero.

178
00:11:27.364 --> 00:11:28.089
Cost:

179
00:11:28.369 --> 00:11:31.783
Higher commit latency; a lost replica blocks commits.

180
00:11:33.133 --> 00:11:36.333
L2 (real PostgreSQL streaming replication):

181
00:11:36.613 --> 00:11:52.101
Async: the standby followed within milliseconds normally, but under a 200,000-row burst the replication lag peaked at ~16 MB of un-replayed WAL, that's the RPO window if the primary is lost at that instant.

182
00:11:52.381 --> 00:11:53.469
Commits are fast.

183
00:11:54.819 --> 00:12:15.299
Sync (synchronousstandbynames): every commit waited for the standby to persist the WAL, so after the primary was killed the standby had every committed row (RPO 0), at the cost of commit latency, and the availability risk that a lost standby blocks commits (mitigated with ANY 2 (...) quorum over multiple standbys).

184
00:12:15.579 --> 00:12:28.379
Read replicas also offload reads (Ch 10), but a replica used for DR must be watched for lag (Ch 24), a badly lagging async replica quietly widens your RPO.

185
00:12:29.729 --> 00:12:30.646
Failover.

186
00:12:30.926 --> 00:12:34.617
Failover promotes a replica to primary when the primary fails.

187
00:12:34.897 --> 00:12:41.019
L2 promoted the standby in ~115 ms, but that's only part of the RTO.

188
00:12:41.299 --> 00:12:43.027
Real failover has stages:

189
00:12:43.307 --> 00:12:51.819
Detection: notice the primary is down (health checks, consensus), and avoid false positives (a network blip isn't a dead primary).

190
00:12:52.099 --> 00:13:03.235
Fencing: ensure the old primary is truly out (STONITH or fencing tokens, Ch 20) to prevent split-brain, two nodes both accepting writes, which corrupts data.

191
00:13:03.515 --> 00:13:05.990
Promotion: a replica becomes primary.

192
00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:15.319
Redirection: clients or DNS or service endpoints point at the new primary (connection re-pointing, Ch 08).

193
00:13:15.599 --> 00:13:21.807
Re-warm: caches and connection pools refill (the new primary starts cold, Ch 25).

194
00:13:22.087 --> 00:13:35.015
Automatic versus manual: automatic (Patroni, Kubernetes operators, cloud RDS Multi-AZ) is fast but risks flapping and split-brain if detection is wrong; manual is safe but slow.

195
00:13:35.295 --> 00:13:40.927
Most use automatic with careful detection (a consensus quorum, Ch 12) and fencing.

196
00:13:42.277 --> 00:13:46.202
Split-brain is the danger: never let two primaries accept writes.

197
00:13:46.482 --> 00:13:52.839
Consensus-based leader election with fencing (Ch 12, Ch 20) is the correct foundation.

198
00:13:53.119 --> 00:14:02.506
Failback: after the old primary returns, it must resync (as a replica of the new primary) before any switch back, never assume its data is current.

199
00:14:03.856 --> 00:14:06.309
Backup verification and restore drills.

200
00:14:06.589 --> 00:14:08.000
A backup is only real if you've restored it.

201
00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:14.571
L4 ran an automated restore drill against three backups that all reported success:

202
00:14:14.851 --> 00:14:18.136
Good backup: restored, row count verified, then usable.

203
00:14:18.416 --> 00:14:25.819
Truncated backup (disk filled mid-copy, the classic silent failure): pgrestore failed, then not a usable backup.

204
00:14:26.099 --> 00:14:38.707
Orphaned backup (restores clean, but a retention job pruned referenced data): restored but the post-restore verification (row count versus an expected manifest) failed, then not usable.

205
00:14:40.057 --> 00:14:41.507
Only the good one passed.

206
00:14:41.787 --> 00:14:58.171
The lesson: schedule automated restore-and-verify drills, alert when one fails, restore into a throwaway environment and check integrity (row counts, checksums, application-level smoke tests), and track the drill's wall-clock time as your real RTO.

207
00:14:58.451 --> 00:15:04.638
Do the same for failover: regular game-day failover drills so the RTO is measured, not hoped.

208
00:15:05.988 --> 00:15:08.121
Multi-region disaster recovery.

209
00:15:08.401 --> 00:15:13.159
A single region can fail entirely (natural disaster, provider outage).

210
00:15:13.439 --> 00:15:17.172
Multi-region patterns trade cost, RPO and RTO:

211
00:15:18.522 --> 00:15:19.866
Here is a quick table:

212
00:15:20.146 --> 00:15:22.919
Pattern, and RTO, and RPO, and Cost.

213
00:15:23.199 --> 00:15:25.610
Backup and restore (to another region).

214
00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:26.807
RTO:

215
00:15:27.087 --> 00:15:27.919
Hours.

216
00:15:28.199 --> 00:15:29.095
RPO:

217
00:15:29.375 --> 00:15:30.634
Since last backup.

218
00:15:30.914 --> 00:15:31.639
Cost:

219
00:15:31.919 --> 00:15:32.773
Cheapest.

220
00:15:33.053 --> 00:15:36.338
Pilot light (minimal standby, scale up on failover).

221
00:15:36.618 --> 00:15:38.666
RTO: ~10s of min.

222
00:15:38.946 --> 00:15:39.842
RPO:

223
00:15:40.122 --> 00:15:41.679
Async replication lag.

224
00:15:41.959 --> 00:15:42.685
Cost:

225
00:15:42.965 --> 00:15:43.690
Low.

226
00:15:43.970 --> 00:15:46.551
Warm standby (scaled-down running copy).

227
00:15:46.831 --> 00:15:47.749
RTO:

228
00:15:48.029 --> 00:15:48.839
Minutes.

229
00:15:49.119 --> 00:15:50.015
RPO:

230
00:15:50.295 --> 00:15:51.277
Async lag.

231
00:15:51.557 --> 00:15:52.282
Cost:

232
00:15:52.562 --> 00:15:53.373
Medium.

233
00:15:53.653 --> 00:15:56.831
Active-active or hot standby (both regions serving).

234
00:15:57.111 --> 00:15:58.029
RTO:

235
00:15:58.309 --> 00:15:59.119
Seconds.

236
00:15:59.399 --> 00:16:00.295
RPO:

237
00:16:00.575 --> 00:16:02.581
Near-zero (sync where feasible).

238
00:16:02.861 --> 00:16:03.586
Cost:

239
00:16:03.866 --> 00:16:04.655
Highest.

240
00:16:06.005 --> 00:16:24.629
The hard part is data, not compute (Ch 10, Ch 11, Ch 26): cross-region replication is high-latency, so synchronous cross-region is usually too slow, most use async cross-region (accepting a small RPO) or per-region data with reconciliation.

241
00:16:24.909 --> 00:16:26.019
Traffic routing:

242
00:16:26.299 --> 00:16:32.784
GeoDNS or global load balancer or anycast, with health-based failover (Ch 08).

243
00:16:33.064 --> 00:16:41.491
Data residency (Ch 36) may forbid replicating some data across regions, a legal constraint on your DR design.

244
00:16:42.841 --> 00:16:44.099
Chaos engineering.

245
00:16:44.379 --> 00:16:58.289
Chaos engineering is the discipline of deliberately injecting failure to verify resilience, you form a hypothesis (a failure in dependency X won't breach our SLO), inject the fault, and check whether it holds.

246
00:16:58.569 --> 00:17:03.945
It turns unknown weaknesses into known, fixed ones before a real incident finds them.

247
00:17:04.225 --> 00:17:14.721
L3 (real HTTP, a non-critical dependency) ran four game-day experiments against a product page, with Ch 18 patterns off versus on:

248
00:17:16.071 --> 00:17:17.415
Here is a quick table:

249
00:17:17.695 --> 00:17:21.684
Fault, and Naive, and Resilient (timeout + breaker + fallback).

250
00:17:21.964 --> 00:17:22.860
Baseline.

251
00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:23.972
Naive:

252
00:17:24.252 --> 00:17:25.340
SLO held.

253
00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:28.372
Resilient (timeout + breaker + fallback):

254
00:17:28.652 --> 00:17:29.740
SLO held.

255
00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:32.175
Dependency slow (+3 s).

256
00:17:32.455 --> 00:17:36.017
Naive: p99 3,006 ms, breached.

257
00:17:36.297 --> 00:17:42.271
Resilient (timeout + breaker + fallback): p99 151 ms, held.

258
00:17:42.551 --> 00:17:44.556
Dependency 50% errors.

259
00:17:44.836 --> 00:17:45.668
Naive:

260
00:17:45.948 --> 00:17:47.953
49% errors, breached.

261
00:17:48.233 --> 00:17:50.985
Resilient (timeout + breaker + fallback):

262
00:17:51.265 --> 00:17:52.652
0%, held.

263
00:17:52.932 --> 00:17:54.191
Dependency crashed.

264
00:17:54.471 --> 00:17:55.303
Naive:

265
00:17:55.583 --> 00:17:57.524
100% errors, breached.

266
00:17:57.804 --> 00:18:00.556
Resilient (timeout + breaker + fallback):

267
00:18:00.836 --> 00:18:02.223
0%, held.

268
00:18:03.573 --> 00:18:18.485
The naive service let a non-critical dependency's failure propagate and breach the whole page's SLO; the resilient one (a tight timeout, a circuit breaker, and an empty-recommendations fallback) held the SLO through every fault.

269
00:18:18.765 --> 00:18:23.074
Chaos engineering is how you know this before an outage proves it otherwise.

270
00:18:23.354 --> 00:18:36.879
Practice: start in staging, form a hypothesis, inject the smallest fault, have a stop button and a small blast radius, then graduate to production game days with care (Netflix's Chaos Monkey pioneered this).

271
00:18:37.159 --> 00:18:46.482
Common faults: kill instances, add latency, drop packets, exhaust CPU or disk, fail a dependency, partition the network (Ch 12).

272
00:18:47.832 --> 00:18:49.219
Graceful degradation.

273
00:18:49.499 --> 00:18:55.899
When something fails, degrade instead of collapsing: shed non-essential features to keep the core journey working.

274
00:18:56.179 --> 00:18:58.653
L4's degradation ladder for checkout:

275
00:18:58.933 --> 00:19:09.323
Core dependencies (catalog, inventory, payment): the purchase cannot complete without them, so they get the strongest resilience (replicas, failover, Ch 18).

276
00:19:10.673 --> 00:19:17.265
Degradable dependencies (recommendations, reviews, personalization): if they fail, hide that feature.

277
00:19:17.545 --> 00:19:25.011
L4: losing all three non-core dependencies still let customers buy, the page was visibly reduced but working.

278
00:19:25.291 --> 00:19:27.702
Only a core dependency took checkout down.

279
00:19:27.982 --> 00:19:41.507
A degradation catalogue decides, per feature, what to drop and in what order before an incident, so under stress the system follows a plan (Ch 18: fallbacks, brownouts, load shedding) rather than falling over.

280
00:19:41.787 --> 00:19:47.611
This is the application-level complement to the infrastructure DR of the rest of the chapter.

281
00:19:48.961 --> 00:19:50.177
Incident response.

282
00:19:50.457 --> 00:19:53.913
Reliability includes how you handle the inevitable incident:

283
00:19:54.193 --> 00:20:01.703
On-call with clear ownership, actionable alerts (Ch 24: page only on SLO burn), and escalation.

284
00:20:01.983 --> 00:20:12.692
Runbooks: tested, step-by-step recovery procedures, kept current because they're used in drills (the §1.1 runbook was wrong because nobody had run it).

285
00:20:12.972 --> 00:20:19.735
Incident command: roles (commander, comms, ops) for a major incident, so it's coordinated, not chaotic.

286
00:20:21.085 --> 00:20:31.922
Blameless postmortems: focus on the systemic causes and the fixes, not individual blame, people are honest only when it's safe, and honesty is how you actually learn.

287
00:20:32.202 --> 00:20:39.946
Error budgets (Ch 24) tie it together: when the budget is spent, reliability work takes priority over features.

