515
00:35:36.997 --> 00:35:41.456
It runs to 147 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

516
00:35:41.736 --> 00:35:52.552
ChaosL3.java, run with: java -Dsun.net.httpserver.nodelay=true ChaosL3.java (JDK 21, no dependencies).

517
00:35:52.832 --> 00:36:07.552
Chaos engineering: inject real faults into a real dependency and measure whether the caller's SLO holds, with the. resilience patterns of Ch 18 (timeout + circuit breaker + fallback) OFF versus ON.

518
00:36:07.832 --> 00:36:09.731
A product service depends on a.

519
00:36:12.581 --> 00:36:13.498
On screen:

520
00:36:13.778 --> 00:36:18.578
Game day: the product page depends on a NON-critical 'recommendations' service.

521
00:36:18.858 --> 00:36:27.583
Hypothesis each time: a failure there must not breach the product-page SLO (p99 300 ms, errors 1%).

522
00:36:27.863 --> 00:37:01.607
We. inject the fault and check. --- resilience patterns OFF (timeout 150ms + breaker + empty-recos fallback) ---. baseline (no fault) naive p99 153 ms errors 0.0% SLO HELD. dependency SLOW (+3 s) naive p99 3012 ms errors 0.0% SLO BREACHED. dependency 50% ERRORS naive p99 6 ms errors 50.2% SLO

523
00:37:01.887 --> 00:37:29.023
BREACHED. dependency CRASHED (conn refused) naive p99 6 ms errors 100.0% SLO BREACHED. recovery (fault cleared) naive p99 3 ms errors 0.0% SLO HELD. --- resilience patterns ON (timeout 150ms + breaker + empty-recos fallback) ---. baseline (no fault) resilient p99 4 ms errors 0.0% SLO HELD.

524
00:37:29.303 --> 00:37:30.817
The rest is in the chapter.

525
00:37:33.667 --> 00:37:34.521
Reading it:

526
00:37:34.801 --> 00:37:48.390
Naive let the non-critical dependency's failure propagate: a 3 s slowdown dragged the page's p99 to 3,006 ms, and errors or crashes pushed the error rate to 49 to 100%.

527
00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:50.441
Every fault breached the SLO.

528
00:37:50.721 --> 00:38:08.555
Resilient, a 150 ms timeout, a circuit breaker, and an empty-recommendations fallback, held the SLO through every fault: the timeout capped the slow case at 151 ms, the breaker stopped hammering the dead dependency, and the fallback kept the page working.

529
00:38:09.905 --> 00:38:14.108
The point of chaos engineering: you form the hypothesis and test it.

530
00:38:14.388 --> 00:38:20.873
The naive service looked fine until the dependency failed, the experiment is how you find that out on your terms.

