00:43:32.437 --> 00:43:33.717
Production Concerns.

577
00:43:34.967 --> 00:43:36.119
Failure modes.

578
00:43:36.399 --> 00:43:37.743
Here is a quick table:

579
00:43:38.023 --> 00:43:41.010
Failure, and Symptom, and Cause, and Mitigation.

580
00:43:41.290 --> 00:43:42.741
Data loss on corruption.

581
00:43:43.021 --> 00:43:43.831
Symptom:

582
00:43:44.111 --> 00:43:45.775
Can't recover mid-day state.

583
00:43:46.055 --> 00:43:46.823
Cause:

584
00:43:47.103 --> 00:43:50.538
Nightly backups only, no PITR (§1.1).

585
00:43:50.818 --> 00:43:51.821
Mitigation:

586
00:43:52.101 --> 00:43:54.703
WAL archiving + PITR (L1).

587
00:43:54.983 --> 00:43:56.349
Backup unrestorable.

588
00:43:56.629 --> 00:43:57.439
Symptom:

589
00:43:57.719 --> 00:44:00.045
Restore fails during a real incident.

590
00:44:00.325 --> 00:44:01.093
Cause:

591
00:44:01.373 --> 00:44:04.189
Never drilled; silent truncation (L4).

592
00:44:04.469 --> 00:44:05.471
Mitigation:

593
00:44:05.751 --> 00:44:07.970
Automated restore-and-verify drills.

594
00:44:08.250 --> 00:44:10.063
Unknown or large RPO.

595
00:44:10.343 --> 00:44:11.154
Symptom:

596
00:44:11.434 --> 00:44:13.567
How much did we lose? unanswerable.

597
00:44:13.847 --> 00:44:14.615
Cause:

598
00:44:14.895 --> 00:44:17.669
Async replica with unwatched lag (L2).

599
00:44:17.949 --> 00:44:18.951
Mitigation:

600
00:44:19.231 --> 00:44:21.621
Monitor lag; sync for critical data.

601
00:44:21.901 --> 00:44:23.522
Slow or failed failover.

602
00:44:23.802 --> 00:44:24.613
Symptom:

603
00:44:24.893 --> 00:44:25.917
Long outage.

604
00:44:26.197 --> 00:44:26.965
Cause:

605
00:44:27.245 --> 00:44:30.103
Manual, unpracticed (§1.1).

606
00:44:30.383 --> 00:44:31.386
Mitigation:

607
00:44:31.666 --> 00:44:34.546
Automated fenced failover + drills (L2).

608
00:44:34.826 --> 00:44:35.829
Split-brain.

609
00:44:36.109 --> 00:44:36.919
Symptom:

610
00:44:37.199 --> 00:44:39.226
Data corruption after failover.

611
00:44:39.506 --> 00:44:40.274
Cause:

612
00:44:40.554 --> 00:44:42.367
Two primaries accepting writes.

613
00:44:42.647 --> 00:44:43.650
Mitigation:

614
00:44:43.930 --> 00:44:47.343
Fencing or consensus (Ch 12, Ch 20).

615
00:44:48.693 --> 00:44:50.549
Region outage takes everything.

616
00:44:50.829 --> 00:44:51.640
Symptom:

617
00:44:51.920 --> 00:44:53.093
Total downtime.

618
00:44:53.373 --> 00:44:54.141
Cause:

619
00:44:54.421 --> 00:44:55.552
Single region.

620
00:44:55.832 --> 00:44:56.835
Mitigation:

621
00:44:57.115 --> 00:45:00.507
Multi-region async DR (§2.7).

622
00:45:00.787 --> 00:45:02.557
Dependency failure cascades.

623
00:45:02.837 --> 00:45:03.648
Symptom:

624
00:45:03.928 --> 00:45:06.701
Whole page down when one service fails (L3).

625
00:45:06.981 --> 00:45:07.749
Cause:

626
00:45:08.029 --> 00:45:10.013
No resilience or degradation.

627
00:45:10.293 --> 00:45:11.296
Mitigation:

628
00:45:11.576 --> 00:45:14.904
Ch 18 patterns + degradation catalogue.

629
00:45:15.184 --> 00:45:16.933
Ransomware encrypts backups.

630
00:45:17.213 --> 00:45:18.024
Symptom:

631
00:45:18.304 --> 00:45:19.819
No clean restore point.

632
00:45:20.099 --> 00:45:20.867
Cause:

633
00:45:21.147 --> 00:45:22.896
Backups writable or online.

634
00:45:23.176 --> 00:45:24.179
Mitigation:

635
00:45:24.459 --> 00:45:27.680
Immutable or air-gapped copies (3 to 2-1).

636
00:45:27.960 --> 00:45:30.179
Retention pruned needed WAL.

637
00:45:30.459 --> 00:45:31.269
Symptom:

638
00:45:31.549 --> 00:45:33.149
PITR can't reach the target.

639
00:45:33.429 --> 00:45:34.197
Cause:

640
00:45:34.477 --> 00:45:36.909
Retention shorter than the recovery window.

641
00:45:37.189 --> 00:45:38.192
Mitigation:

642
00:45:38.472 --> 00:45:40.499
Align retention to the PITR window.

643
00:45:41.849 --> 00:45:44.067
Observability (Ch 24).

644
00:45:44.347 --> 00:45:50.662
Replication lag in bytes and time, a widening async lag is a widening RPO; alert on it.

645
00:45:50.942 --> 00:45:59.667
Backup and restore-drill success (both, a green backup with a red drill is a false sense of safety), and the drill's measured RTO trend.

646
00:45:59.947 --> 00:46:04.470
Failover-drill RTO over time; detection latency; fencing events.

647
00:46:04.750 --> 00:46:10.233
Chaos-experiment results as first-class artifacts (hypothesis, outcome, fixes).

648
00:46:10.513 --> 00:46:17.275
Error budget or SLO dashboards (Ch 24) that drive when reliability work preempts features.

649
00:46:18.625 --> 00:46:19.564
Security.

650
00:46:19.844 --> 00:46:34.692
Backups are a prime target and a prime leak: encrypt them at rest and in transit (Ch 23), access-control the backup store, and keep an immutable or air-gapped copy against ransomware (which increasingly targets backups first).

651
00:46:34.972 --> 00:46:46.471
DR must respect data residency (Ch 36): some data legally cannot be replicated to another region, a constraint on the DR topology, not an afterthought.

652
00:46:47.821 --> 00:47:00.642
Failover and restore are privileged operations: audit them, and ensure the DR path doesn't bypass your security controls (a restored DB must come up with the same access controls, not open).

653
00:47:00.922 --> 00:47:08.559
Test restores in isolation: a restored copy with production data is production data, same protection, or scrubbed.

654
00:47:09.909 --> 00:47:11.573
Performance and tuning knobs.

655
00:47:11.853 --> 00:47:13.197
Here is a quick table:

656
00:47:13.477 --> 00:47:14.693
Knob, and Guidance.

657
00:47:14.973 --> 00:47:17.789
Backup frequency + WAL archive:

658
00:47:18.069 --> 00:47:20.544
Sets the PITR window or RPO floor.

659
00:47:20.824 --> 00:47:22.659
Sync versus async or quorum:

660
00:47:22.939 --> 00:47:28.400
Sync (or ANY N) for RPO 0 on critical data; async for latency elsewhere.

661
00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:30.365
Replica count and placement:

662
00:47:30.645 --> 00:47:34.379
More replicas or regions = better DR, more cost.

663
00:47:34.659 --> 00:47:35.981
Failover detection:

664
00:47:36.261 --> 00:47:41.403
Fast enough for RTO, slow or consensus enough to avoid false failovers.

665
00:47:41.683 --> 00:47:43.069
Restore parallelism:

666
00:47:43.349 --> 00:47:46.656
Parallel restore (pgrestore -j) to cut RTO.

667
00:47:46.936 --> 00:47:52.077
Retention: ≥ your PITR or recovery window and compliance needs.

668
00:47:53.427 --> 00:47:54.153
Cost.

669
00:47:54.433 --> 00:48:03.606
RPO or RTO cost money non-linearly: near-zero RPO (sync, multi-region hot standby) is far dearer than a few minutes.

670
00:48:03.886 --> 00:48:08.771
Tier it, pay for strict targets only where the data warrants (payments), not everywhere.

671
00:48:09.051 --> 00:48:20.827
Idle DR capacity is the cost of insurance: pilot-light or warm-standby trade RTO for cost; active-active is dearest but also serves traffic (so it isn't pure waste).

672
00:48:21.107 --> 00:48:27.315
Drills cost time but prevent the eleven-hour outage (§1.1) whose cost dwarfs them.

673
00:48:27.595 --> 00:48:31.457
The cheapest reliability investment is usually the restore drill.

674
00:48:32.807 --> 00:48:33.745
Part 7.

675
00:48:34.025 --> 00:48:35.732
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

676
00:48:36.982 --> 00:48:37.622
1.

677
00:48:37.902 --> 00:48:42.595
Untested backups (L4: truncated and orphaned backups both succeeded).

678
00:48:42.875 --> 00:48:48.315
Schedule automated restore-and-verify drills; a backup you've never restored is a hope.

679
00:48:48.595 --> 00:48:49.299
2.

680
00:48:49.579 --> 00:48:51.798
Nightly full backups only, no PITR.

681
00:48:52.078 --> 00:48:54.531
A mid-day disaster loses up to a day.

682
00:48:54.811 --> 00:48:57.777
Add continuous WAL archiving (L1).

683
00:48:58.057 --> 00:48:58.803
3.

684
00:48:59.083 --> 00:49:01.302
Unmonitored async replication lag.

685
00:49:01.582 --> 00:49:04.078
Your RPO silently grows with the lag.

686
00:49:04.358 --> 00:49:08.262
Alert on lag in time and bytes (L2, Ch 24).

687
00:49:08.542 --> 00:49:09.246
4.

688
00:49:09.526 --> 00:49:14.219
Manual, unpracticed failover (§1.1: an hour of googling).

689
00:49:14.499 --> 00:49:17.038
Automate it (with fencing) and drill it monthly.

690
00:49:18.388 --> 00:49:19.156
5.

691
00:49:19.436 --> 00:49:21.996
Failover without fencing, then split-brain.

692
00:49:22.276 --> 00:49:23.940
Two primaries corrupt data.

693
00:49:24.220 --> 00:49:27.975
Use consensus + fencing (Ch 12, Ch 20).

694
00:49:28.255 --> 00:49:29.001
6.

695
00:49:29.281 --> 00:49:32.929
Assuming the old primary's data is current after it returns.

696
00:49:33.209 --> 00:49:37.668
It must resync as a replica before any failback; never switch back blind.

697
00:49:37.948 --> 00:49:38.695
7.

698
00:49:38.975 --> 00:49:40.105
Single region.

699
00:49:40.385 --> 00:49:42.199
A region can fail entirely.

700
00:49:42.479 --> 00:49:47.172
Have a cross-region DR plan sized to the business (§2.7).

701
00:49:47.452 --> 00:49:48.135
8.

702
00:49:48.415 --> 00:49:51.316
No graceful degradation (L3, L4).

703
00:49:51.596 --> 00:49:58.935
One dependency failure shouldn't take the whole page down; define a degradation catalogue and use Ch 18 patterns.

704
00:50:00.285 --> 00:50:01.031
9.

705
00:50:01.311 --> 00:50:03.935
Backups online and writable (ransomware bait).

706
00:50:04.215 --> 00:50:08.205
Keep immutable or air-gapped copies; follow 3 to 2-1.

707
00:50:08.485 --> 00:50:09.189
10.

708
00:50:09.469 --> 00:50:11.901
Retention shorter than the recovery window.

709
00:50:12.181 --> 00:50:18.431
If retention prunes the WAL or backups you'd need to reach a recovery point, PITR can't get there.

710
00:50:18.711 --> 00:50:19.607
Align them.

711
00:50:19.887 --> 00:50:20.719
11.

712
00:50:20.999 --> 00:50:22.877
Testing DR only on paper.

713
00:50:23.157 --> 00:50:26.869
A runbook nobody executes is fiction (§1.1).

714
00:50:27.149 --> 00:50:28.066
Game-day it.

715
00:50:28.346 --> 00:50:29.135
12.

716
00:50:29.415 --> 00:50:32.338
Chaos in prod with no blast-radius control.

717
00:50:32.618 --> 00:50:38.869
Start in staging, small faults, a stop button, and a hypothesis, chaos engineering, not chaos.

718
00:50:40.219 --> 00:50:41.093
Part 8.

719
00:50:41.373 --> 00:50:42.717
Interview Q and A.

720
00:50:43.967 --> 00:50:44.799
Beginner.

721
00:50:45.079 --> 00:50:46.103
Question 1.

722
00:50:46.383 --> 00:50:48.197
What are RPO and RTO?

723
00:50:48.477 --> 00:50:49.842
Thirty-second answer:

724
00:50:50.122 --> 00:50:57.781
RPO (Recovery Point Objective) is the maximum data loss you'll accept, in time, at most 5 minutes of orders.

725
00:50:58.061 --> 00:51:04.589
RTO (Recovery Time Objective) is the maximum time to restore service, back within 30 minutes.

726
00:51:04.869 --> 00:51:11.973
RPO is set by backup frequency and replication; RTO by restore speed and failover automation.

727
00:51:12.253 --> 00:51:13.767
You tier them per data type.

728
00:51:15.117 --> 00:51:16.120
Question 2.

729
00:51:16.400 --> 00:51:18.192
Why isn't 'we have backups' enough?

730
00:51:18.472 --> 00:51:19.837
Thirty-second answer:

731
00:51:20.117 --> 00:51:32.277
Because a backup you've never restored may not be restorable, it can be silently truncated, corrupted, encrypted the wrong way, or missing the data it references, and the backup job still reports success.

732
00:51:32.557 --> 00:51:35.309
Only a restore drill proves recoverability.

733
00:51:35.589 --> 00:51:41.413
And a nightly full backup alone means losing up to a day; you also need point-in-time recovery.

734
00:51:42.763 --> 00:51:43.809
Question 3.

735
00:51:44.089 --> 00:51:45.859
What is point-in-time recovery?

736
00:51:46.139 --> 00:51:47.505
Thirty-second answer:

737
00:51:47.785 --> 00:52:04.339
Restoring a database to a specific moment by combining a base (full) backup with a continuous archive of the write-ahead log: restore the backup, then replay the WAL up to a chosen timestamp or named point, for example, the instant before a bad DELETE.

738
00:52:04.619 --> 00:52:07.926
It gives a tiny RPO without backing up constantly.

739
00:52:09.276 --> 00:52:10.343
Question 4.

740
00:52:10.623 --> 00:52:14.911
Synchronous versus asynchronous replication, what's the difference for DR?

741
00:52:15.191 --> 00:52:16.556
Thirty-second answer:

742
00:52:16.836 --> 00:52:28.484
Synchronous: a commit waits until the replica has persisted the change, so if the primary dies you lose nothing (RPO 0), but commits are slower and a lost replica can block writes.

743
00:52:28.764 --> 00:52:40.476
Asynchronous: the primary commits immediately and streams to the replica in the background, so commits are fast but a sudden primary loss loses whatever was still in flight (RPO 0).

744
00:52:41.826 --> 00:52:42.829
Intermediate.

745
00:52:43.109 --> 00:52:44.218
Question 5.

746
00:52:44.498 --> 00:52:47.442
Design a backup strategy for the orders database.

747
00:52:47.722 --> 00:52:49.087
Thirty-second answer:

748
00:52:49.367 --> 00:52:59.117
Continuous WAL archiving + periodic base backups, then point-in-time recovery, so you can recover to any second within the retention window.

749
00:52:59.397 --> 00:53:09.658
Store copies per 3 to 2-1 (3 copies, 2 media, 1 off-site), encrypted, with at least one immutable or air-gapped copy against ransomware.

750
00:53:09.938 --> 00:53:14.311
Retention ≥ the recovery window and compliance needs.

751
00:53:14.591 --> 00:53:19.669
And, non-negotiable, automated restore drills that verify and measure RTO.

752
00:53:21.019 --> 00:53:22.128
Question 6.

753
00:53:22.408 --> 00:53:25.992
Walk through a database failover and how you avoid split-brain.

754
00:53:26.272 --> 00:53:27.637
Thirty-second answer:

755
00:53:27.917 --> 00:53:44.024
Detect the primary is down (via consensus or quorum, not a single probe, to avoid false positives); fence the old primary so it can't accept writes; promote a replica to primary; repoint clients or DNS to it; let caches or pools re-warm.

756
00:53:44.304 --> 00:53:51.941
Split-brain, two primaries both writing, is the danger, and fencing (or consensus-based leadership) is what prevents it.

757
00:53:53.291 --> 00:53:54.379
Question 7.

758
00:53:54.659 --> 00:53:57.753
What is chaos engineering and how do you start safely?

759
00:53:58.033 --> 00:53:59.398
Thirty-second answer:

760
00:53:59.678 --> 00:54:14.910
Deliberately injecting failure to verify resilience: you state a hypothesis (a failure in X won't breach our SLO), inject the fault, and check if it holds, turning unknown weaknesses into known, fixed ones before a real incident.

761
00:54:15.190 --> 00:54:24.342
Start in staging, with the smallest fault, a small blast radius, a stop button, and good observability; graduate to production game days with care.

762
00:54:25.692 --> 00:54:26.716
Question 8.

763
00:54:26.996 --> 00:54:30.580
What is graceful degradation, and how do you decide what to shed?

764
00:54:30.860 --> 00:54:32.225
Thirty-second answer:

765
00:54:32.505 --> 00:54:38.393
When a dependency fails, drop the non-essential feature it powers instead of failing the whole page.

766
00:54:38.673 --> 00:54:53.692
You decide with a degradation catalogue: classify each dependency as core (the journey can't complete without it) or degradable (hide the feature, use a fallback), and define what to shed and in what order, before an incident forces the choice.

767
00:54:55.042 --> 00:54:56.109
Question 9.

768
00:54:56.389 --> 00:54:58.607
How do you measure and improve RTO?

769
00:54:58.887 --> 00:55:00.253
Thirty-second answer:

770
00:55:00.533 --> 00:55:12.138
Measure it by drilling, run an actual restore or failover in a realistic environment and time it end to end (detection, then restore or promote, then redirect, then verify).

771
00:55:12.418 --> 00:55:25.495
Improve it by automating failover, parallelizing restores, keeping a warm or hot standby instead of restoring from cold backups, pre-warming caches, and keeping runbooks current because they're used in drills.

772
00:55:26.845 --> 00:55:27.613
Senior.

773
00:55:27.893 --> 00:55:28.939
Question 10.

774
00:55:29.219 --> 00:55:33.848
Design DR for the grocery platform with tiered RPO or RTO.

775
00:55:34.128 --> 00:55:35.493
Thirty-second answer:

776
00:55:35.773 --> 00:55:37.459
Tier by data criticality.

777
00:55:37.739 --> 00:55:47.275
Payments or orders: synchronous replication (RPO ~0) + automated fenced failover (RTO 2 min) + PITR for corruption.

778
00:55:47.555 --> 00:55:54.104
Inventory or catalog: async replication + PITR (RPO 1 min, RTO 5 min).

779
00:55:54.384 --> 00:55:58.565
Analytics: nightly backups, rebuild from source (RPO 1 h).

780
00:55:58.845 --> 00:56:03.347
Whole-region loss: async cross-region replicas promoted on failover.

781
00:56:03.627 --> 00:56:06.976
Prove all of it with automated restore and failover drills.

782
00:56:08.326 --> 00:56:09.457
Question 11.

783
00:56:09.737 --> 00:56:12.254
Your async replica is your DR copy.

784
00:56:12.534 --> 00:56:14.625
What can go wrong and how do you guard it?

785
00:56:14.905 --> 00:56:16.270
Thirty-second answer:

786
00:56:16.550 --> 00:56:17.531
Three things:

787
00:56:17.811 --> 00:56:34.430
(1) it can lag, silently widening your RPO, monitor lag in time and bytes and alert; (2) it faithfully replicates a bad DELETE or corruption, so it's not a substitute for PITR; (3) failover can hit split-brain if the old primary isn't fenced.

788
00:56:34.710 --> 00:56:40.171
Guard with lag alerts, PITR alongside replication, and fenced or consensus failover.

789
00:56:41.521 --> 00:56:42.609
Question 12.

790
00:56:42.889 --> 00:56:45.855
How do you run a restore drill, and what does it verify?

791
00:56:46.135 --> 00:56:47.500
Thirty-second answer:

792
00:56:47.780 --> 00:57:04.164
On a schedule (nightly for critical DBs), automatically restore the latest backup into a throwaway environment, then verify: the restore itself succeeds, row counts or checksums match an expected manifest, and an application-level smoke test passes.

793
00:57:04.444 --> 00:57:08.775
Alert if any step fails, and record the wall-clock as your measured RTO.

794
00:57:09.055 --> 00:57:10.804
Never restore over production.

795
00:57:12.154 --> 00:57:13.434
Question 13.

796
00:57:13.714 --> 00:57:16.807
Design a chaos engineering program for the platform.

797
00:57:17.087 --> 00:57:18.453
Thirty-second answer:

798
00:57:18.733 --> 00:57:25.581
Start with a hypothesis tied to an SLO (a recommendations outage won't breach the product-page SLO).

799
00:57:25.861 --> 00:57:35.695
Begin in staging with small, controlled faults (latency, errors, instance kill) and a stop button; verify observability catches it; fix what breaks.

800
00:57:35.975 --> 00:57:42.482
Graduate to scheduled production game days with a small blast radius, during business hours with the team watching.

801
00:57:42.762 --> 00:57:47.711
Automate the recurring experiments (for example, a Chaos Monkey killing instances).

802
00:57:49.061 --> 00:57:50.256
Question 14.

803
00:57:50.536 --> 00:57:54.141
What is split-brain and how do consensus and fencing prevent it?

804
00:57:54.421 --> 00:57:55.787
Thirty-second answer:

805
00:57:56.067 --> 00:58:06.349
Split-brain is two nodes both believing they're the primary and both accepting writes, after a network partition or a bad failover, which diverges and corrupts data.

806
00:58:06.629 --> 00:58:22.203
Prevent it by electing a single leader through consensus (a quorum must agree, Ch 12) and by fencing the old primary (revoking its ability to write, STONITH or a fencing token the storage checks, Ch 20) before promoting a new one.

807
00:58:23.553 --> 00:58:24.449
Architect.

808
00:58:24.729 --> 00:58:25.923
Question 15.

809
00:58:26.203 --> 00:58:28.614
How do you design for a full region outage?

810
00:58:28.894 --> 00:58:30.259
Thirty-second answer:

811
00:58:30.539 --> 00:58:46.689
Run a second region and pick a pattern by RPO or RTO or cost: backup-restore (hours), pilot light (minimal standby, scale up on failover), warm standby (scaled-down running copy), or active-active (both serve, near-zero RTO).

812
00:58:46.969 --> 00:58:59.150
Replicate data cross-region asynchronously (sync is too slow across regions), route with GeoDNS or global LB with health-based failover, and respect data-residency limits.

813
00:58:59.430 --> 00:59:01.243
Then drill the region failover.

814
00:59:02.593 --> 00:59:03.788
Question 16.

815
00:59:04.068 --> 00:59:08.420
Replication gives fast failover; PITR gives point-in-time recovery.

816
00:59:08.700 --> 00:59:09.916
Why do you need both?

817
00:59:10.196 --> 00:59:11.561
Thirty-second answer:

818
00:59:11.841 --> 00:59:13.783
They defend against different failures.

819
00:59:14.063 --> 00:59:22.831
Replication protects against infrastructure failure, a dead primary or zone, with a fast failover to a live copy (short RTO).

820
00:59:23.111 --> 00:59:28.551
But a replica faithfully copies logical damage: a bad DELETE, an app bug, corruption.

821
00:59:28.831 --> 00:59:32.777
PITR protects against those by recovering to a point before the damage.

822
00:59:33.057 --> 00:59:37.473
A replica can't undo a dropped table; PITR can't fail over in milliseconds.

823
00:59:37.753 --> 00:59:38.735
You need both.

824
00:59:40.085 --> 00:59:41.429
Question 17.

825
00:59:41.709 --> 00:59:44.738
Design an incident-response practice for the platform.

826
00:59:45.018 --> 00:59:46.383
Thirty-second answer:

827
00:59:46.663 --> 01:00:06.781
Actionable, SLO-based alerts (Ch 24), then on-call with clear ownership and escalation, then incident command roles (commander, comms, ops) for majors, then tested runbooks (kept current by drills), then mitigate first, root-cause later, then blameless postmortems focused on systemic fixes,

828
01:00:07.061 --> 01:00:12.714
then error budgets (Ch 24) that make reliability work preempt features when spent.

829
01:00:12.994 --> 01:00:16.898
Practice with game days so the muscle exists before the real incident.

830
01:00:18.248 --> 01:00:19.421
Question 18.

831
01:00:19.701 --> 01:00:23.605
How do error budgets connect reliability to product velocity?

832
01:00:23.885 --> 01:00:25.251
Thirty-second answer:

833
01:00:25.531 --> 01:00:32.144
An SLO defines an acceptable failure rate; the error budget is the allowed amount (Ch 24).

834
01:00:32.424 --> 01:00:34.941
While there's budget, teams ship features freely.

835
01:00:35.221 --> 01:00:44.757
When the budget is spent, the policy flips: reliability work (fixing the causes, hardening, DR) takes priority over features until the budget recovers.

836
01:00:45.037 --> 01:00:49.581
It makes reliability a shared, quantified decision instead of an argument.

837
01:00:50.931 --> 01:00:52.169
Question 19.

838
01:00:52.449 --> 01:00:56.395
What's your rollout plan to bring a legacy service up to this DR bar?

839
01:00:56.675 --> 01:00:58.041
Thirty-second answer:

840
01:00:58.321 --> 01:00:59.579
In order of impact:

841
01:00:59.859 --> 01:01:06.835
(1) turn on WAL archiving + PITR and run a restore drill (you may find, like §1.1,

842
01:01:07.115 --> 01:01:26.657
that current backups don't restore); (2) add a monitored replica and measure the async RPO; (3) automate and drill failover with fencing; (4) write a degradation catalogue and add Ch 18 patterns to the top dependencies; (5) add a cross-region replica; (6) start chaos game days.

843
01:01:26.937 --> 01:01:29.774
Each step is independently valuable and shippable.

844
01:01:31.124 --> 01:01:32.233
Question 20.

845
01:01:32.513 --> 01:01:36.311
How does reliability and DR tie together the rest of this book?

846
01:01:36.591 --> 01:01:37.956
Thirty-second answer:

847
01:01:38.236 --> 01:01:59.121
It's where the pieces are proven under failure: replication (Ch 10) sets the RPO; consensus or fencing (Ch 12, Ch 20) makes failover safe; resilience patterns (Ch 18) and degradation keep the app up during partial failure (L3 or L4); observability (Ch 24) sets SLOs,

848
01:01:59.401 --> 01:02:11.305
error budgets and the signals DR depends on; deployment (Ch 26) provides multi-region and rollback; and chaos engineering validates all of it before an incident does.

849
01:02:12.655 --> 01:02:13.551
Part 9.

850
01:02:13.831 --> 01:02:15.239
Summary and Practice.

851
01:02:16.489 --> 01:02:17.577
Key takeaways.

852
01:02:17.857 --> 01:02:26.924
RPO and RTO are the targets; everything else (backups, replication, failover) exists to meet them, tiered by data criticality.

853
01:02:27.204 --> 01:02:34.671
PITR (base backup + WAL archiving) recovers from corruption or human error to a point in time (L1:

854
01:02:34.951 --> 01:02:38.023
57,000 rows back, RPO 0).

855
01:02:38.303 --> 01:02:40.436
A backup you've never restored is a hope.

856
01:02:40.716 --> 01:02:44.151
Automated restore drills catch silent failures (L4).

857
01:02:44.431 --> 01:02:52.708
Sync replication = RPO 0 at latency cost; async = fast commits with a lag-sized RPO (L2).

858
01:02:52.988 --> 01:02:54.076
Monitor the lag.

859
01:02:55.426 --> 01:03:01.314
You need both a replica and PITR, replication copies logical corruption; PITR undoes it.

860
01:03:01.594 --> 01:03:09.210
Failover must fence the old primary to avoid split-brain (Ch 12, Ch 20), and be practiced (L2).

861
01:03:09.490 --> 01:03:16.082
Multi-region for region loss, async cross-region, sized by RPO or RTO or cost.

862
01:03:16.362 --> 01:03:23.402
Chaos engineering proves resilience before an outage does (L3: naive breached every fault; resilient held).

863
01:03:23.682 --> 01:03:29.314
Graceful degradation keeps the core journey working while shedding non-core features (L4).

864
01:03:30.664 --> 01:03:38.088
Incident response, runbooks, drills, blameless postmortems, error budgets, is part of reliability, not separate from it.

865
01:03:39.438 --> 01:03:40.761
Decision cheat sheet.

866
01:03:41.041 --> 01:03:42.385
Here is a quick table:

867
01:03:42.665 --> 01:03:44.179
Situation, and Choice.

868
01:03:44.459 --> 01:03:46.678
Recover from a bad DELETE or corruption:

869
01:03:46.958 --> 01:03:48.174
PITR to before it.

870
01:03:48.454 --> 01:03:50.481
Hardware or zone primary failure:

871
01:03:50.761 --> 01:03:52.510
Replica failover (fenced).

872
01:03:52.790 --> 01:03:55.265
Zero data loss required (ledger):

873
01:03:55.545 --> 01:03:57.678
Synchronous or quorum replication.

874
01:03:57.958 --> 01:04:00.262
Latency-sensitive, some loss OK:

875
01:04:00.542 --> 01:04:02.846
Async replication + monitor lag.

876
01:04:03.126 --> 01:04:04.342
Whole-region loss:

877
01:04:04.622 --> 01:04:06.862
Cross-region async replica, promote.

878
01:04:07.142 --> 01:04:08.401
Prove backups work:

879
01:04:08.681 --> 01:04:10.899
Automated restore-and-verify drills.

880
01:04:11.179 --> 01:04:12.609
Prove resilience works:

881
01:04:12.889 --> 01:04:14.147
Chaos game days.

882
01:04:14.427 --> 01:04:16.113
Partial dependency failure:

883
01:04:16.393 --> 01:04:19.529
Degradation catalogue + Ch 18 patterns.

884
01:04:20.879 --> 01:04:22.095
Prevent split-brain:

885
01:04:22.375 --> 01:04:23.889
Consensus + fencing.

886
01:04:25.239 --> 01:04:26.370
Exercises.

887
01:04:26.650 --> 01:04:27.994
Here is a quick table:

888
01:04:28.274 --> 01:04:30.130
Band, and Exercise, and Hint.

889
01:04:30.410 --> 01:04:31.285
B2.

890
01:04:31.565 --> 01:04:32.631
Exercise:

891
01:04:32.911 --> 01:04:40.079
Extend L1 to recover to a recoverytargettime 5 seconds before the delete instead of a named point.

892
01:04:40.359 --> 01:04:41.895
Hint: timestamp target.

893
01:04:42.175 --> 01:04:43.050
B2.

894
01:04:43.330 --> 01:04:44.397
Exercise:

895
01:04:44.677 --> 01:04:52.591
In L2, add a second standby and configure ANY 1 (s1,s2) sync; kill one standby and show commits continue.

896
01:04:52.871 --> 01:04:54.109
Hint: quorum sync.

897
01:04:54.389 --> 01:04:55.263
B3.

898
01:04:55.543 --> 01:04:56.610
Exercise:

899
01:04:56.890 --> 01:05:04.421
Add a network-partition fault to L3 (drop the dependency's packets, not crash) and see if the timeout still saves you.

900
01:05:04.701 --> 01:05:06.535
Hint: iptables or latency.

901
01:05:06.815 --> 01:05:07.690
B3.

902
01:05:07.970 --> 01:05:09.037
Exercise:

903
01:05:09.317 --> 01:05:14.970
Add an application smoke test to L4's restore drill (place an order in the restored DB).

904
01:05:15.250 --> 01:05:16.871
Hint: end-to-end verify.

905
01:05:17.151 --> 01:05:18.026
B4.

906
01:05:18.306 --> 01:05:19.373
Exercise:

907
01:05:19.653 --> 01:05:29.317
Build a region-failover simulation: two regions with async replication, kill region 1, promote region 2, measure RPO or RTO.

908
01:05:29.597 --> 01:05:31.133
Hint: cross-region lag.

909
01:05:32.483 --> 01:05:33.357
B4.

910
01:05:33.637 --> 01:05:34.704
Exercise:

911
01:05:34.984 --> 01:05:40.744
Write the degradation catalogue for the home, search and checkout pages with drop order and fallbacks.

912
01:05:41.024 --> 01:05:42.773
Hint: core versus droppable.

913
01:05:44.123 --> 01:05:45.126
Further reading.

914
01:05:45.406 --> 01:05:53.150
Google, Site Reliability Engineering and The SRE Workbook (SLOs, error budgets, incident response, postmortems).

